{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~6–12 EUR), especially when the recent history is in the low-80s but the market transitions to low-70s.",
    "Regime-change/turning-point lag: after a local peak (around 82–84), the model continues to project elevated levels for 1–3+ weeks instead of capturing the sharp downshift; apply a faster downward adjustment when the last 5–10 days show weakening.",
    "Insufficient downside volatility: realized paths exhibit step-down moves and sustained lower plateaus; the model smooths and underestimates drawdowns. Add a drawdown amplifier when short-term momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: forecast error grows with horizon (mid-to-late window shows the largest overprediction). Use an increasing negative correction with lead time (e.g., subtract more for days 10–30 than days 1–5).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean reversion: model repeatedly predicts rebounds back toward ~80–85 after dips, but realized prices do not revert that quickly. Damp mean-reversion strength and cap rebound speed following declines.",
    "Spike/plateau overshoot: when the recent history includes high prints (80+), the model anchors to them and overshoots subsequent levels. Reduce anchoring weight on the top quartile of the last-30-day history when momentum is negative."
  ]
}
